  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Tuesday...\n\nFog is possible this morning, mainly over the creek and river \nvalleys of eastern Montana and north-central Wyoming (20 percent \nchance). Any fog that does develop will dissipate through the \nmorning hours, giving way to a seasonable day with high \ntemperatures in the 40s and 50s (F) under mostly sunny skies. \nTonight, temperatures will drop into the 20s and 30s (F) with the \npotential for fog over the plains of eastern Montana and north- \ncentral Wyoming once again (20 percent chance). \n\nThursday will be a slightly warmer day with high temperatures in \nthe 50s to near 60 degrees (F). While mostly dry conditions will \nprevail, a shortwave trough is expected to pass through the region\nlate Thursday into early Friday, bringing a slight chance of \nprecipitation to south-central Montana (15 to 30 percent chance). \nWith this, precipitation type is expected to be rain over the \nlower elevations and snow in the mountains. Ahead of this \nshortwave trough, breezy winds may also develop around Livingston \nto Nye Thursday morning, with gusts in the 20s and 30s (mph) \nforecast.\n\nA ridge of high pressure looks to return Friday into Saturday \nahead of another potential weather system passing to the north \nlate Sunday into Monday. Ahead of this system, winds are expected \nto increase Friday into Sunday over the mountains and foothills of\nsouth-central Montana, strongest around Livingston to Nye through\nBig Timber and Harlowton. While the conditions look most \nfavorable for Big Timber to Harlowton Friday into Saturday, the \nmost favorable conditions around Livingston to Nye are likely to \noccur late Saturday into Sunday. With this, periods of wind gusts \nin the 20s to 40s (mph) look to be common, with occasional gusts \nto 50 mph around Livingston to Nye Sunday morning. As far as the \nchance of seeing a 60+ mph gust goes, the chance is 25 percent \naround Livingston to Nye Sunday morning.\n\nEnsembles suggest warm and mainly dry conditions will take hold \nnext week under a ridge of high pressure aloft. LA",
